Project Goal:
Build reliable, secure, and maintainable software with clean, readable, production-ready code.

Development Rules:

### Highest Priority (Must Always Follow)
- Always understand the existing codebase before making any changes.
- Always create a `TODO.md` file before starting any task and update it after each completed task using checkboxes (`[ ]` / `[x]`).
- Work on one task at a time. Implement, verify, and test in small, manageable chunks. Do not perform large changes or testing in a single step.
- Explain significant changes before implementing them.
- Preserve the existing project structure unless explicitly instructed otherwise.
- Strictly follow the MVC architecture and request flow:
  `HTML → JavaScript → Routes → Controllers → Models → Database`
- Never write business logic in Routes.
- Never call Models directly from Routes.
- Never write SQL queries in Controllers. All database operations must be handled through Models.
- Never write JavaScript or CSS inside HTML files.
- Never make API calls directly from HTML. API calls must be made only from the corresponding JavaScript file.
- Always use centralized CSS files and centralized reusable UI components.
- Do not write or append any `write_to_file` keyword in any generated file.

### High Priority
- Prefer simple, maintainable, and readable solutions over complex implementations.
- Review the database schema (tables and columns) before writing or modifying database queries.
- When creating APIs, name them according to the corresponding page URL or module.
- Keep API naming consistent across the project.
- Use centralized reusable components for:
  - HTML Tables
  - Date Filters
  - Dropdown Filters
  - Pagination
  - Common UI Components
- Build professional, consistent, and user-friendly interfaces.
- Reuse existing components whenever possible.
- Keep changes isolated to the requested feature only.

### Code Quality
- Write clean, modular, and readable code.
- Follow the existing coding standards and project conventions.
- Avoid unnecessary dependencies.
- Keep functions focused on a single responsibility.
- Avoid duplicate code (DRY Principle).
- Add comments only when they improve understanding.
- Do not modify unrelated code.

### Testing
- Verify each completed feature before proceeding to the next task.
- Check for syntax, runtime, and logical errors before marking a task as completed.
- Ensure new changes do not break existing functionality.
- Mark tasks as completed in `TODO.md` only after successful verification.